  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nAll indications continue to point towards keeping the heavier rain \nto the south later this afternoon and tonight. The high resolution \nmodels only bring about a quarter of an inch of rain to southern \nparts of Woodbury and Ida counties with the ensemble output showing \nabout a 20-40 percent chance to exceed 1 inch of rain, the EC \nprobabilities a little higher. With 3-4 inches of rain needed to \nmake any if the rivers really move upwards, flooding concerns are \nvery minimal.\n\nFog will again be possible later tonight into Thursday morning, but \nfor now the model soundings suggest the lower level saturated layer \nis much shallower, so a better chance to burn off more quickly in \nthe morning. A surge of drier air will build southward with dew \npoints falling into the 40s through the morning. Also looks like the \nbetter chances would be over parts of northwest IA and nearby \nlocations.\n\nA system will move by to the north Friday into Saturday. While this \nwill bring some mid level cloud cover into the area, precipitation \nchances will remain fairly low with dry air below about 800 mb. Some \nvery weak instability aloft Friday night into Saturday ahead of an \nincoming shot of cooler and drier air may see the need to add some \nspotty showers during this time, but confidence fairly low in this \nweak instability. Without the weak instability, saturation looks to \noccur below the dgz so rainfall would not be expected without the \ninstability. Overall impacts will be very minor.\n\nUpper level ridging builds into the area Sunday into Monday ahead of \nthe next wave. This wave will bring small chances for showers Monday \nnight into Tuesday, but again very low confidence fighting drier air \nwith the wave well to the north. One thing that this wave will bring \nis mild weather to the area. Looking at highs Tuesday and Wednesday \naround 70 in most locations. Highs more in the lower to mid 60s are \nexpected this weekend.",
